<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anagni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anagnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anagni, ancient Anagnia, is a hill town in the Sacco valley south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was the leading city of the Hernici. Rome punished it after its revolt of 306 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a favourite residence of the popes, and in 1303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> was seized there by agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>.</p></div>
